Yes—etymologically they are close relatives.

* Sense comes through French sens from Latin sensus, from sentīre: “to feel or perceive.”
* Scent came through French sentir: “to feel, perceive, or smell”—also from Latin sentīre. English originally spelled it sent; the silent c was implanted centuries later for uncertain reasons. Merriam-Webster: scent⁠￼, sense⁠￼

So scent is a specialized branch of sense: broad perception narrowed into olfactory perception.

And entomologically, your accidental spelling works beautifully: ants and other insects convert scent into collective sense using pheromone trails. One insect deposits information into the environment; another senses it later.

A scent is a sense sent outside the body.

That is external memory, stigmergy, and semantic hyperlinking in one mechanism.

Even better, the silent c is linguistic titanium: a foreign piece added later, functionally naturalized into the word without changing its voice.

Scent is sense with an orthographic implant.